MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m05",
  "summary": "Explain how engine lubrication and cooling systems work, recognise their faults, and use compression, leak-down, vacuum and pressure tests to diagnose mechanical engine problems.",
  "outcomes": ["Describe the oil circuit, the oil pump, the pressure relief valve and the four jobs that oil does.","Explain oil contamination, wear and the PCV system, and what milky, fuel-diluted or sludged oil tells you.","Describe the parts of the cooling system and why a coolant mixture and a pressure cap matter.","Explain how a wax thermostat works and the symptoms of one stuck open or stuck closed.","Diagnose overheating, low oil pressure and head gasket failure using the correct tests.","Carry out and interpret a compression test, a leak-down test and a vacuum gauge test."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Oil does four jobs: lubricates, cools, cleans and seals.","If the oil pressure warning light comes on, stop the engine at once and find the cause.","A wax thermostat opens at a set temperature, usually between about 80 and 95 °C.","Never open the radiator cap on a hot engine. The system is pressurised.","Coolant is a mix of antifreeze and water, typically about half and half. Follow the manual.","Thermostat stuck open: slow warm-up. Stuck closed: fast overheating.","Compression test: warm engine, all spark plugs out, throttle open, compare each cylinder.","A wet test adds a little oil. If the reading rises, the piston rings are worn. If not, the valves are leaking.","A leak-down test shows the percentage of pressure lost and, by sound, where it escapes."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m05-l1", "title": "The lubrication system", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An engine's moving parts would destroy each other in seconds without oil. The lubrication system delivers clean oil under pressure to every bearing and sliding surface. This lesson explains how it works."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"oil-circuit"},
      {"t":"h","text":"What oil does"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Lubricates:** forms a film that keeps metal surfaces apart.","**Cools:** carries heat away from pistons, bearings and valve gear.","**Cleans:** holds soot and fine dirt in suspension until the filter removes it.","**Seals:** fills the tiny gap between the piston rings and the cylinder wall."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The oil circuit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Oil sits in the **sump**. A **pickup tube** with a strainer draws it into the **oil pump**, which is driven by the crankshaft or the timing drive. The pump pushes oil through the **oil filter** and into the **main oil gallery**, a long drilled passage in the block. From the gallery, smaller passages lead to the main bearings, then through drillings in the crankshaft to the big-end bearings. Other passages feed the camshaft, the valve gear, and sometimes the timing chain and piston-cooling jets. The oil then drains back to the sump by gravity."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pump, relief valve and bypass"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Common pump designs are **gear** pumps and **rotor** (gerotor) pumps. Both are positive-displacement pumps, so pressure rises with engine speed. A **pressure relief valve** opens at a set pressure and returns surplus oil to the sump, so a cold, thick oil cannot burst the filter or seals. The **filter bypass valve** opens if the filter is blocked or the oil is very thick, so the engine is not starved of oil. Unfiltered oil is better than no oil, but this is only a safety back-up."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The oil pressure warning"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **oil pressure warning light** (or gauge) tells you the oil pressure has dropped below a safe minimum. It does not show how much oil is in the sump. If it comes on while driving, stop safely and switch off the engine. Running an engine with low oil pressure can wreck the bearings in a few minutes. Check the oil level first, then find the cause."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Cause of low oil pressure","Typical sign"],"rows":[["Low oil level or a leak","Dipstick reads low, oil under the vehicle"],["Worn bearings","Low pressure when warm, knocking noises"],["Worn or faulty oil pump","Low pressure at all speeds"],["Blocked pickup strainer or stuck relief valve","Low pressure that follows a long period of poor maintenance"],["Wrong grade of oil or fuel in the oil","Thin oil, smell of fuel on the dipstick"]],"caption":"Low oil pressure: common causes"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Measuring oil pressure step by step"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the oil level and condition first. Low or thin oil gives a false low reading.","Remove the oil pressure switch or sender, catch the drips, and screw the test gauge adapter into the same hole.","Start the engine and read the pressure at cold idle, then let it reach working temperature.","Read the pressure at hot idle, and then at a higher speed such as 3,000 rpm. Compare each with the manual.","Fit the switch with a new washer or sealant as the manual says, and check for leaks."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Condition","Typical reading","What it means"],"rows":[["Cold idle","Higher than hot, perhaps 3 to 4 bar","Thick oil. The relief valve may open."],["Hot idle","At least the minimum in the manual, often about 1 bar","Shows bearing and pump condition"],["Hot, 3,000 rpm","About 3 to 5 bar","Shows the relief valve and pump output"]],"caption":"Specifications differ between engines. Always use the manual."},
      {"t":"p","text":"A pressure that is low at hot idle but recovers as the speed rises usually points to worn bearings, because oil leaks out through the clearances faster than the pump can supply it at low speed. A pressure that is low at all speeds points to the pump, the pickup, the relief valve or the oil itself."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"An oil pressure warning light that flickers at idle when hot is a warning. Do not ignore it. Check the oil level and have the pressure measured with a gauge, as the manual describes."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"To check pressure properly, remove the oil pressure switch and fit a mechanical gauge in its place. Compare the reading with the manual at idle and at a higher speed, both warm and cold."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m05-l2", "title": "Oil pumps, coolers and lubrication components", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The lubrication system is more than a pump and a filter. It has oil coolers, baffles, relief valves and pressure sensors, and on some engines extra systems for turbochargers and variable valve timing. Knowing what each part does helps you find a fault quickly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil pumps"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The pump sits in or on the block or the timing cover, and is driven by the crankshaft or a chain. A **gear pump** has two meshing gears that carry oil round between the teeth and the housing. A **gerotor pump** has an inner rotor with one tooth fewer than the outer ring, and it carries oil in the changing gaps between them. A **vane pump** has sliding vanes in a rotor. Worn pumps lose pressure, so most pumps have a flat cover with a clearance that can be measured with a feeler gauge and a straight edge."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Variable displacement pumps** change their output to match the engine's need. At low speed the pump delivers a full flow, and at high speed it reduces flow to save the power the pump uses. The control is oil pressure acting on a spring, or an electric valve. These pumps depend on the correct oil and a clean pickup."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pressure relief valve"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Oil pumps move more oil as engine speed rises, so a spring-loaded **relief valve** opens at a set pressure and returns the excess to the sump or the pump inlet. A valve stuck open gives low pressure. A valve stuck closed raises pressure and can burst the filter or blow out seals. Typical working pressure on a warm engine is about 1 to 2 bar at idle and 3 to 5 bar at higher speed, but check the manual for the engine you are working on."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Filters and bypass"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The oil filter removes particles as small as a few thousandths of a millimetre. A **non-return valve** stops oil draining out of the filter when the engine stops, so that oil reaches the bearings quickly at the next start. A **bypass valve** opens when the filter is blocked or the oil is thick and cold. A filter without a proper anti-drain valve causes a noisy start-up."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil coolers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Hot oil thins and protects less, so many engines have an **oil cooler**. A small one is an oil-to-water heat exchanger mounted by the filter, and a larger one is an oil-to-air radiator in the air stream. A leak in an oil-to-water cooler can let coolant into the oil, or oil into the coolant, and both are serious. Look for milky oil, an oily film in the coolant tank and a loss of coolant."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil pickup, baffles and sump design"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["The **pickup tube** with a strainer sits low in the sump. A blocked strainer starves the pump, and a cracked pipe draws in air.","A **windage tray** or baffle stops the spinning crankshaft from whipping the oil into foam and keeps oil at the pickup in corners.","A **dry sump** system, used in sports cars, keeps the oil in a separate tank and uses a scavenge pump to pull it out of a shallow sump."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Turbochargers and variable valve timing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A turbocharger spins up to 200,000 rpm or more and needs a continuous oil supply and a free-flowing drain. A restrictor in the feed line protects it from too much oil. A blocked or kinked drain pipe makes oil leak into the turbine. Variable valve timing phasers use oil pressure to move the camshaft, and tiny filters in the control valve can block with dirt. Faults in these systems often show as oil pressure faults, rattles at start-up or camshaft codes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil pressure sensors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An **oil pressure switch** is a simple on-off switch that lights the warning lamp below a set pressure, often only 0.3 to 0.5 bar. An **oil pressure sender** gives a variable signal for a gauge or the computer. The light coming on tells you the pressure is very low, but it does not tell you the pressure is normal when it is off. A gauge test with a mechanical gauge is the only way to be sure."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Oil pressure alone does not show good lubrication. Oil level, oil condition, bearing clearance and the pump all work together. Check each of them."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never run an engine for long without oil pressure, and never rev a cold engine hard. Oil is thick when cold, and it takes a few seconds to reach the top of the engine."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m05-l3", "title": "Oil, wear and contamination", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Oil is not a permanent fluid. It works hard, and it picks up everything the engine puts into it. Reading the condition of the oil tells you a great deal about the engine."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Viscosity and additives"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Viscosity** is how easily a fluid flows. Oil that is too thick flows slowly when cold, so parts are starved at start-up, and when it is too thin it does not keep the metal apart. Oil contains **additives** that help it resist heat, foam, oxidation and wear, and that keep dirt suspended. These additives are used up, which is why oil must be changed at the interval in the schedule."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How oil degrades"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Heat breaks oil down and makes it thicker and darker. Short trips are hard on oil because the engine never gets hot enough to burn off water and fuel that get into it. Dust that gets past the air filter, soot and combustion by-products add to the contamination. Over time, oil forms **sludge**, a thick black deposit that blocks passages and strainers."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil analysis"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A sample of used oil can be sent to a laboratory for **oil analysis**. The report lists wear metals, such as iron from cylinders and crankshaft, copper and lead from bearings and aluminium from pistons, and shows the amount of fuel, coolant, soot and dirt in the oil. It also reports the remaining additive reserve and the viscosity. Trends over several samples are more useful than a single result. Fleets and heavy-duty operators use analysis to decide on oil change intervals and to catch a failure early."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Finding","What it suggests"],"rows":[["High iron","Wear of cylinders, rings, camshaft or crankshaft"],["High copper or lead","Bearing wear or a leaking oil cooler"],["High aluminium","Piston wear or scuffing"],["Silicon (dirt)","Dirt getting past the air filter or a leak in the intake"],["Sodium or potassium","Coolant contamination"],["Fuel above a few per cent","Injector leak, rich running, or too many cold starts"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What the oil tells you"},
      {"t":"table","head":["On the dipstick","Possible cause"],"rows":[["Milky, light brown","Coolant is mixing with the oil, for example a failed head gasket or cracked head"],["Thin, smells of fuel","Fuel is leaking into the oil, from rich running, leaking injectors or too many cold starts"],["Black and gritty","Very old oil or poor filtering"],["Glittering metal flakes","Bearing or other metal wear. Needs investigation"],["Level slowly drops","Oil is being burned or is leaking"]],"caption":"Reading oil condition"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The PCV system"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some combustion gas always leaks past the piston rings into the crankcase. This gas is called **blow-by**. The **positive crankcase ventilation (PCV)** system pulls it out of the crankcase and into the intake to be burned. A blocked PCV valve or hose raises crankcase pressure and can force oil out of seals and gaskets, or cause oil burning and sludge. Check and clean or replace it at the interval in the manual."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil consumption"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some oil use is normal, especially in a high-mileage or high-speed engine, but heavy use is a fault. The oil can leak out externally, or be burned inside the engine because of worn piston rings, worn valve stem seals or a blocked PCV system. Blue smoke from the exhaust shows oil is burning."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Measuring oil consumption"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Change the oil and filter and fill to the full mark, on level ground. Record the odometer reading.","Drive the vehicle normally for a set distance, for example 1,000 km.","Return, park on level ground, wait the same time as before and check the level on the dipstick.","Add measured oil, a little at a time, to reach the full mark again. The amount added is the consumption for that distance."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Compare the result with the maker's allowance, which differs between engines. Anything above it points to leaks or burning. Look for external leaks first, then check the PCV system, the valve seals, rings and bores. A turbocharger with worn seals also burns oil, which shows as blue smoke or oil in the intake pipes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Sludge and engine flushing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If an engine has been neglected, the inside may be coated with sludge. Sludge blocks the pickup strainer and oil passages and can starve a bearing. Prevention is better than cure: use the right oil, change it on time, keep the PCV system working and avoid constant short trips. If sludge is present, a gentle flush and several short-interval oil changes may help. Harsh flushing chemicals can loosen deposits that then block passages, so follow the maker's advice. Sometimes dismantling and cleaning is the only safe cure."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Always note when you check the oil and how much you add. A record of how much oil is used between services helps to decide whether the engine is getting worse."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m05-l4", "title": "The cooling system", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Only about a third of the energy in the fuel becomes useful work. Much of the rest becomes heat. The cooling system removes that heat to keep the engine at a stable working temperature, and it also provides heat for the cabin."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"cooling-circuit"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Water pump:** driven by a belt or the timing drive, it circulates coolant through the engine and radiator.","**Radiator:** a heat exchanger. Hot coolant flows through thin tubes, and air passing over the fins carries the heat away.","**Thermostat:** a valve that controls the coolant flow so that the engine warms up quickly and then stays at the right temperature.","**Expansion tank:** holds spare coolant, lets the coolant expand when hot, and carries the pressure cap.","**Hoses:** connect the parts. They are flexible so that the engine can move on its mountings.","**Cooling fan:** pulls air through the radiator when the vehicle is slow or stopped. It may be driven electrically or by the engine, and is switched on by a temperature sensor or the computer.","**Heater matrix:** a small radiator in the cabin. Coolant flows through it to heat the air."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Coolant"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Coolant is a mixture of water and **antifreeze** (ethylene glycol or similar), often mixed about half and half, but follow the manual and the maker's instructions. Antifreeze lowers the freezing point, raises the boiling point and contains **corrosion inhibitors** that protect the metal parts. Plain water boils too easily, rusts the engine and can freeze in cold places. Never mix different types of coolant, as they can react and form deposits."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The pressure cap"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The cooling system is sealed and held under pressure by the **pressure cap**. Pressure raises the boiling point of the coolant, so the engine can run hot without boiling. A typical cap holds roughly 0.9 to 1.4 bar (13 to 20 psi), and its rating is stamped on it. A weak cap lets the coolant boil and escape."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never open the radiator cap or the expansion tank cap when the engine is hot. The coolant is above its normal boiling point and will spray out. Let the engine cool, then open the cap slowly in stages, using a cloth."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Heat from the heater"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If the cabin heater blows cold air while the engine is hot, coolant may not be flowing through the heater matrix. Common causes are low coolant, an air lock or a blocked matrix. If the heater is blowing warm only when the engine is revved, the coolant flow is poor."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Operating temperature"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The coolant in a modern engine normally runs at about 85 to 105 °C. Metal parts near the combustion chamber are far hotter, and oil in the sump is usually a little hotter than the coolant. The temperature gauge shows the coolant temperature, often with a wide zone in the middle so it does not move for small changes. Running too cool leaves fuel and moisture in the oil, wastes fuel and makes more pollution. Running too hot thins the oil, causes knock, warps the head and can seize the pistons."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Liquid and air cooling"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Most car engines are liquid cooled because water jackets carry heat away evenly and quietly. An **air-cooled** engine has fins on the cylinders and head and a large fan to blow air over them. It has no coolant to leak or freeze, but it runs hotter and noisier, and it is harder to keep at an even temperature. The air-cooled engine depends on clean fins and clear ducts. A blocked or missing cowling can cause rapid overheating. You will meet air-cooled engines on some older cars, motorcycles and small equipment."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Heat expansion in a cooling system","steps":["A cooling system holds 7.0 litres of coolant. Coolant expands by about 4% between cold and fully hot.","Expansion = 7.0 × 0.04 = 0.28 litres.","The expansion tank must have room for at least this much extra coolant, or the system will push coolant out of the overflow pipe each time it warms up. A level that is too high when cold is a common cause of unexplained coolant loss."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A cooling system that needs regular topping up has a leak. Look for wet or crusty patches at hose joints, the radiator, the water pump and the heater matrix. A pressure test makes small leaks show up."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m05-l5", "title": "Cooling system components and heat transfer", "minutes": 6, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Cooling is a problem of moving heat. Heat has to travel from the combustion chamber, through the metal, into the coolant, round to the radiator and out into the air. If any step in the chain fails, the engine overheats. This lesson looks at the parts in detail and at the numbers behind the system."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How much heat is there?"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a typical petrol engine about a third of the fuel energy ends up in the coolant, about the same amount as the engine delivers as power. A car producing 60 kW at the crankshaft sends about 50 to 60 kW of heat into the coolant, as much as a large electric fire. The radiator has to get rid of it, even on a hot day while the car crawls in traffic."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Heat (kW) = coolant flow (kg/s) × specific heat (kJ/kg·K) × temperature rise (K)","caption":"The specific heat of a 50/50 coolant mixture is about 3.5 kJ/kg·K, lower than water at 4.2"},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much coolant must the pump move?","steps":["The engine sends 50 kW of heat into the coolant. The coolant should rise by no more than 8 °C as it passes through the engine.","Flow = 50 ÷ (3.5 × 8) = 1.79 kg/s.","Coolant has a density of about 1.05 kg per litre, so 1.79 ÷ 1.05 = 1.7 litres per second, or about 102 litres per minute.","If the pump impeller is worn, the belt slips or a hose collapses, the flow falls and the temperature rise grows. The engine then runs hotter at the same load."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The radiator"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A radiator has a core of thin tubes joined by fine fins. Hot coolant flows through the tubes and air passes between the fins. Most modern radiators are aluminium with plastic tanks, and a few older types are copper and brass. The tanks are crimped to the core with a rubber seal, and a common failure is a cracked tank or a leaking seam. In a **cross-flow** radiator the coolant flows from side to side, which suits low bonnets. Check the fins for bending, corrosion and a blockage of dirt, leaves or insects. Check the **air flow** as well. Missing air ducts, shrouds and undertrays reduce cooling, so refit them after repair."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The pressure cap"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The cap has two valves. The **pressure valve** opens at the cap rating to release excess pressure into the expansion tank. The **vacuum valve** opens when the system cools and contracts, to let coolant flow back from the tank and prevent the hoses from collapsing. A weak spring or a worn seal means the system cannot hold pressure, so the boiling point falls and coolant is lost. At a cap rating of 1 bar the boiling point of a 50/50 mixture is about 125 °C. Replace the cap if you doubt it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Water pump"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The water pump is a centrifugal pump. Its impeller throws coolant outward, and the pump housing guides it to the engine block. The impeller may be steel, cast iron, aluminium or plastic. The pump has a bearing and a seal. A leak from the small **weep hole** in the housing shows a failed seal. A grinding or whining noise means a worn bearing. A pump driven by the timing belt should be replaced with the belt. Some engines use an **electric water pump** controlled by the computer. It can run when the engine is off to cool a hot turbocharger or battery."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Hoses and clamps"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Hoses perish from the inside, so a hose can look good outside yet be soft or cracked inside. Squeeze it. A soft or very hard hose, a bulge or a split is a fault.","The **lower hose** of the radiator often has a coil spring inside to stop it collapsing under pump suction. If it collapses at high speed, the engine overheats and then recovers.","Clamps can be worm-drive, spring or crimped. A spring clamp has to be released with pliers and must go back exactly. Never use a worm clamp where the maker fitted a spring type.","Oil contamination softens rubber. If a hose is soft and oily, find and fix the oil leak."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cooling fans"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it works","Typical faults"],"rows":[["Engine-driven viscous fan","A fan on the water pump shaft, with a fluid clutch that locks up when hot air passes over it","A clutch that does not lock gives overheating in traffic. One that is always locked is noisy and wastes power."],["Electric fan on a relay","A temperature switch or the computer closes a relay to the motor","Blown fuse, bad relay, faulty switch or sensor, or a worn motor"],["Electric fan with variable speed","The computer varies the speed with a PWM control module","Faults in the module, sensors or wiring set codes"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Heater matrix and bypass"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Coolant flows through a small radiator inside the cabin, the **heater matrix**, to warm the air. A blocked or leaking matrix gives a cold heater or damp carpet and a sweet smell. Many engines have a **bypass** passage so that coolant circulates inside the engine when the thermostat is shut. On modern engines, electric valves and pumps send coolant where it is needed so the engine and cabin warm up faster."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Cooling is a chain of heat transfer. A fault at any step, whether coolant flow, air flow, pressure or temperature control, gives the same symptom: overheating. Test each step in order."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Use an infrared thermometer to check temperatures along the radiator and hoses. A radiator that has a large temperature drop from top to bottom is working. A cool patch means a blocked tube."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m05-l6", "title": "Thermostat and temperature control", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An engine runs best within a narrow temperature range. If it is too cold, it wears faster, uses more fuel and makes more pollution. If it is too hot, it can be badly damaged. The thermostat and its control system keep the temperature steady."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"thermostat-operation"},
      {"t":"h","text":"How the wax thermostat works"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **wax thermostat** has a small chamber of wax around a plunger. When the coolant is cold the wax is solid, and a spring holds the valve closed. The coolant then circulates only inside the engine, which warms it up quickly. As the temperature rises, the wax melts and expands, pushing the valve open. Hot coolant then flows to the radiator. The thermostat opens at a set temperature, usually between about 80 and 95 °C, and the temperature is stamped on it. Use the correct rating."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Faults"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","Symptoms"],"rows":[["Stuck closed","Overheating soon after starting, upper radiator hose stays cool while the engine is hot, then very hot and hard"],["Stuck open","Slow warm-up, temperature gauge reads low, weak heater, poor fuel economy"],["Opens late or at the wrong temperature","Temperature swings up and down"]],"caption":"Thermostat faults"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Temperature sensors, gauge and fans"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **coolant temperature sensor** tells the engine computer and the gauge how hot the engine is. The computer uses it to set fuel quantity and to switch on the fan. If the sensor reads wrongly, the engine can run rich or the fan can run all the time, so a faulty sensor may look like a cooling fault. Check the sensor reading with a scan tool or a meter against the actual temperature."},
      {"t":"p","text":"On vehicles with an electric fan, a relay or the computer switches it on above a set temperature, often 90 to 100 °C. A fan that never starts will lead to overheating in traffic, but not on the open road. Test by running the engine until it reaches temperature and watching for the fan to start."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Most coolant temperature sensors are **thermistors**, resistors whose resistance changes with temperature. In a negative temperature coefficient (NTC) type, the resistance falls as the engine warms. The computer supplies a reference voltage through a fixed resistor and measures the voltage across the sensor to find the temperature."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Coolant temperature","Typical NTC sensor resistance","Signal voltage (5 V reference)"],"rows":[["20 °C","About 2,500 Ω","About 3.3 V"],["60 °C","About 600 Ω","About 1.4 V"],["90 °C","About 240 Ω","About 0.7 V"]],"caption":"Values are typical. Use the resistance table in the manual for the exact sensor."},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a sensor wire is open circuit, the computer sees a very high resistance and thinks the engine is very cold. If the wire is shorted to earth, the computer sees a very low resistance and thinks the engine is very hot. Each sets a code."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing a thermostat on the vehicle"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Start the engine from cold, with the cooling system full, and check the temperature with a scan tool or an infrared thermometer on the thermostat housing.","Feel the upper radiator hose. It should stay cool at first, then warm up and become hot soon after the engine reaches the opening temperature, as the thermostat opens.","If the hose heats up straight away, the thermostat is stuck open or missing. If the engine overheats and the hose stays cool, it is stuck closed.","Check the temperature at which the fan starts. A thermostat that opens late lets the temperature overshoot before it settles."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Electronically controlled thermostats"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many modern engines have a thermostat with a small electric heater in the wax. The computer can heat the wax to open the valve early, which lowers the engine temperature under heavy load and keeps it higher when driving gently, saving fuel. If the heater circuit fails, the thermostat still works as a normal wax thermostat but with less control, and the computer sets a code. Check the electrical connection and supply, and replace the whole thermostat housing if the manual requires it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A modern engine should reach normal temperature within a few minutes of driving, and it should then stay steady. A gauge that moves up and down a lot, or never rises, points to the thermostat or the sensor."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"To test a thermostat, hang it in a pan of water on a stove with a thermometer. Watch the temperature at which it starts to open, and compare it with the value stamped on it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m05-l7", "title": "Cooling and lubrication faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Faults in the cooling and lubrication systems are among the most expensive engine problems when missed. This lesson gives you an order of working and the main tests."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Overheating"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Overheating means the engine temperature is rising above normal. Common causes are low coolant, a leak, a faulty thermostat, a failed fan or relay, a blocked radiator (inside or out), a faulty water pump or a loose belt, an air lock, or a weak pressure cap. A head gasket failure can also cause overheating because combustion gas fills the cooling system. If the temperature gauge rises, switch off the air conditioning, put the heater on full to draw heat out, and stop as soon as it is safe."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Finding the cause of overheating"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Check","If the result is","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Coolant level (engine cold)","Low","External or internal leak, or the system was not bled"],["Fan operation at temperature","Fan does not start","Fuse, relay, temperature switch, sensor or fan motor"],["Upper hose temperature","Cool when the engine is hot","Stuck thermostat or no coolant flow"],["Radiator surface","Cool patches","Blocked tubes inside the radiator"],["Radiator fins","Clogged with dirt or insects","Poor air flow"],["Pressure test","Pressure drops","External leak or internal leak (head gasket)"],["Combustion gas in the coolant","Test positive","Head gasket or cracked head"],["Belt and water pump","Slack or noisy","Loose belt or failing pump"]],"caption":"Work through the checks in order, starting with the simplest."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Bleeding air"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Air trapped in the cooling system causes hot spots and poor heater output. After draining and refilling, follow the manual's bleeding procedure. This usually involves a bleed screw or running the engine with the expansion tank cap off, the heater on hot and the engine at fast idle, topping up as the air escapes. Take care that coolant does not spray."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pressure testing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **cooling system pressure tester** pumps air into a cold system through the filler to the pressure of the cap rating. The pressure should hold steady for several minutes. A falling pressure means a leak. Look for external drips, and if none are seen, suspect an internal leak such as a head gasket. A second adaptor tests the pressure cap itself."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Pressure test","steps":["The cap is stamped 1.1 bar (16 psi).","With the engine cold, fit the tester and pump to 1.1 bar. Never go above the cap rating.","Watch the gauge for two minutes. A drop means a leak.","Check the hoses, radiator, water pump and heater joints for drips and wet marks."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Head gasket failure"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Bubbles in the coolant, or coolant pushed out of the expansion tank under acceleration.","White smoke from the exhaust with a sweet smell, and a falling coolant level without an external leak.","Milky oil, or oil in the coolant.","A **combustion gas test** (chemical block test) that changes colour when exhaust gas is in the coolant."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Low oil pressure diagnosis"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Check the oil level and condition. If these are good, check the oil pressure with a mechanical gauge, then check the oil pickup strainer and relief valve, then the pump. If the pressure is low only when warm, suspect worn bearings."},
      {"t":"h","text":"After an overheat"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An engine that has overheated may be damaged even if it seems to run well afterwards. Aluminium heads warp and head gaskets fail. Pistons can scuff and seize. Oil breaks down and loses its protection. After any serious overheating, let the engine cool completely before you open the cap. Then check the coolant level and condition, the oil for signs of coolant, and the compression and leak-down values. Look for gasket leaks and check the head flatness if the engine runs rough. Tell the customer honestly if the engine has been damaged and may need major repairs."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never pour cold water into a very hot engine. The sudden temperature change can crack the block or the head. Switch off, wait for it to cool, and top up slowly."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never run an engine with a known low oil pressure or an overheating condition for a test. A short run can write off the engine."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Fix the cause, not the symptom. Topping up coolant or oil without finding the loss only delays the failure."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m05-l8", "title": "Cooling system repair procedures", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Cooling system repairs are common and rewarding, but they must be done carefully. A system that is not bled properly, or a hose that is not fitted properly, can cause another overheat soon after the job. This lesson gives step-by-step methods for the main repairs."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing a thermostat"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Let the engine cool. Drain enough coolant to bring the level below the thermostat housing, and collect it in a clean container if you will reuse it, or for disposal.","Remove the hose and housing bolts. Note the way the thermostat sits, because it must go back with the correct side to the engine and the small **jiggle valve** or air bleed at the top.","Remove the old thermostat. Clean the seat and the housing face and remove all old gasket.","Fit the new thermostat with a new gasket or O-ring, and check its temperature rating.","Refit the housing and tighten to the torque in the manual. Do not over-tighten, as many housings are plastic or aluminium.","Refill and bleed the system, run the engine to temperature, and check that the thermostat opens and that there are no leaks."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing a water pump"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Drain the coolant and disconnect the battery. Remove the drive belt and any covers.","If the pump is behind the timing belt, set the engine to the timing position and take off the belt following the timing belt procedure in module 4.","Remove the pump bolts and the pump. Clean the sealing face thoroughly, because corrosion on the face causes leaks.","Compare the old and new pump to be sure that the impeller type, size and hole positions match.","Fit the new pump with its gasket or seal, tighten the bolts evenly in a crossing pattern to the torque in the manual.","Refit the timing belt and covers, refill with coolant, bleed the system and test for leaks. Replace the thermostat and belt at the same time if the schedule calls for it."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing hoses"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Release the clamps and twist the hose gently to free it. Do not cut into the metal spigot. If the hose is stuck, slit it lengthways with a knife, taking care not to score the pipe.","Clean the spigots with a wire brush or cloth so that the new hose seals.","Fit the new hose, check that it does not rub on belts, pulleys or sharp edges, and position the clamps just behind the spigot bead, as the maker fitted them.","Moulded hoses must be the exact part, and bendy hoses should not be kinked."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Flushing and refilling"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If the coolant is dirty or the radiator is partly blocked, a flush helps. Drain the system, close the drains and refill with water, run the engine until the thermostat opens and drain again. Repeat until the water runs clear. A **reverse flush** pushes water in the opposite direction to the normal flow and shifts deposits from the radiator tubes. Use a flushing chemical only as the maker advises. Refill with the correct coolant and distilled water mixture, and bleed all the air."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Removing and refitting a radiator"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Drain the coolant. Disconnect the battery and the fan wiring or fan assembly.","Remove the hoses. If the radiator has transmission cooler lines, clamp or plug them and catch the fluid. If the air conditioning condenser is attached, unbolt it from the radiator and tie it up without opening the refrigerant circuit.","Unbolt the radiator and lift it out carefully, without damaging the fins or the condenser.","Transfer any rubber mounts, shrouds and sensors to the new radiator and check that they are correct.","Refit it, refill with coolant, bleed the system, top up the transmission fluid if it was lost, and test for leaks."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Final checks"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Bring the engine to full temperature and check that the fan starts and stops.","Squeeze the upper hose and check that the thermostat is opening.","Check the heater output. Cool air on a hot engine means an air pocket.","Check the level again when the engine is cold.","Check the pressure cap and test the system under pressure.","Dispose of the old coolant at an approved collection point. It is poisonous."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never open a hot cooling system, and never leave coolant where animals or children can reach it. Its sweet taste attracts them and it is poisonous."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Photograph hose routes and clamp positions before you take anything off. Many engines have several hoses of similar shape and different function."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m05-l9", "title": "Mechanical diagnosis", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Mechanical tests measure the condition of the engine itself: how well each cylinder seals. They are the best way to tell if an engine is worn out before you spend money on other repairs."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Compression test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **compression test** measures the pressure each cylinder produces when the engine is cranked. Warm the engine, switch it off, and disable the fuel and ignition. Remove all the spark plugs, open the throttle fully and fit the tester to cylinder 1. Crank the engine for four to five compression strokes and note the highest reading. Repeat on each cylinder. Specifications vary, so use the manual's figure. A healthy petrol engine is typically between about 10 and 14 bar (145 to 200 psi), and the cylinders should agree within about 10 to 15%."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wet test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If one cylinder is low, squirt a small amount of oil into it and repeat the test. If the reading rises noticeably, the oil is sealing worn **piston rings**. If it stays low, the problem is usually a leaking **valve** or **head gasket**. If two neighbouring cylinders are low, suspect the gasket between them."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Compression result","Most likely cause"],"rows":[["All cylinders low and about equal","Worn rings and bores overall, or valve timing that is wrong (a slipped belt or stretched chain)"],["One cylinder low. Wet test raises the reading","Worn or stuck piston rings on that cylinder"],["One cylinder low. Wet test does not change it","A leaking or burnt valve, or a damaged seat"],["Two adjacent cylinders low","Head gasket leaking between them"],["Readings above the specification","Carbon build-up in the chamber, or a thin head gasket or a machined head"],["Readings rise slowly over several strokes","Worn rings, or a slow cranking speed from a weak battery or starter"]],"caption":"Always compare readings with the manual and with each other."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Leak-down test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **leak-down test** feeds regulated compressed air into each cylinder with the piston at top dead centre on the compression stroke. The gauge shows the percentage of air that escapes. Listen to find where it goes: hissing at the exhaust pipe means a leaking exhaust valve, hissing at the intake or throttle body means a leaking intake valve, bubbles in the coolant mean a head gasket leak, and air at the oil filler means worn rings. As a rough guide, under 10% is good, 20% is wearing, and above 30% is poor. Check the tester's instructions."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Vacuum gauge"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **vacuum gauge** on the intake manifold shows how well the engine breathes. A healthy engine gives a steady reading at idle, typically somewhere between about 45 and 70 kPa below atmospheric (13 to 21 inHg) at sea level. A needle that flutters, drops regularly or wanders points to valves, ignition or leaks, and a low steady reading can mean late valve timing or an air leak."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Needle behaviour at idle","Likely meaning"],"rows":[["Steady at normal reading","Healthy engine"],["Steady but low","Late valve or ignition timing, or an air leak"],["Regular drop to a low value","A burnt or sticking valve"],["Rapid vibration, worse as speed rises","Weak valve springs or worn valve guides"],["Slow drift up and down","Mixture or idle control problem"],["Falls steadily as the engine speed is held up","A restricted exhaust or blocked catalytic converter"]],"caption":"Vacuum falls with height above sea level, roughly 3 kPa for each 300 m."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Noises and smoke"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Knock:** a deep rhythmic knock that gets worse under load can be a worn big-end or main bearing.","**Tap or tick:** light, fast, matched to engine speed, usually valve clearance or a faulty hydraulic tappet.","**Slap:** a hollow slap when cold that fades as the engine warms is typical piston slap.","**Blue smoke:** oil burning. **Black smoke:** too much fuel. **White smoke or steam:** coolant burning, or condensation that clears when warm."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"A cylinder power balance test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"With the engine at a steady idle, a scan tool or a simple method is used to cut the ignition or injector to each cylinder in turn, and the change in engine speed is noted. A healthy cylinder gives a noticeable drop when it is cut out. A weak or dead cylinder gives little or no change. This quickly shows which cylinder is not working, and compression, leak-down and ignition tests then show why. On modern engines use the scan tool's own misfire counters or cylinder contribution test, and never shorten a spark lead to earth, as it can damage coils."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Using the results together"},
      {"t":"p","text":"No single test proves a diagnosis. A compression test shows if a cylinder seals, and a leak-down test shows where the leak is. A vacuum gauge gives an overall view, and a power balance test finds the weak cylinder. Use the tests together, compare each result with the manual, and let the evidence lead you. Write each reading on the job card so that the customer can see why you recommend a repair."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Work in a logical order: ask the customer, check fluid levels, look and listen, then test. Do not replace parts to see what happens."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m05-l10", "title": "Mechanical diagnosis case studies", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Reading about tests is not the same as using them. These case studies show how a technician works from the customer's complaint to a confirmed fault, step by step. Notice that each case follows the same pattern: listen, look, test the simplest things first, then use test results to narrow the search."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case 1: A weak, misfiring engine"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A customer reports that a four-cylinder petrol car has lost power and shakes at idle. The warning light is on."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["The fault code reader shows a misfire on cylinder 3. The plugs and coil are checked and are in good order. Swapping the coil with cylinder 2 leaves the misfire on cylinder 3, so the coil is not the cause.","A compression test gives 12, 12, 6 and 12 bar. Cylinder 3 is low.","A little oil is put in cylinder 3 and the test is repeated. The reading remains at 6 bar, so the rings are not the cause.","A leak-down test shows 45% leakage, with a hiss heard at the exhaust pipe. The exhaust valve on cylinder 3 is leaking.","The head is removed. The exhaust valve on cylinder 3 is found burnt on its edge. The valve and seat are reconditioned, the stem seals are replaced, and the engine is rebuilt with a new head gasket.","After the job, compression reads 12 bar on all cylinders and the misfire has gone."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case 2: Overheating that comes and goes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An older car overheats in slow traffic but runs normally on the open road. The coolant level is correct."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["The technician runs the engine at idle and watches the temperature. At 100 °C the fan has still not started.","The fan is energised directly using a fused jumper wire and runs well, so the motor is good.","The relay and fuse are tested and are good. The fault is in the temperature switch signal. The sensor is checked against a thermometer reading and shows a wrong value. The sensor is replaced.","The fan now starts at the correct temperature, and the car stays cool in traffic."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"The customer was told the fault was found by testing, not guessing, and the old sensor was shown to them. Notice that overheating only in slow traffic points to a cooling air problem rather than a coolant flow problem, because road speed drives air through the radiator by itself."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case 3: Low oil pressure when hot"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An engine has a flickering oil pressure light when hot at idle, but the light goes out when the engine speed is raised. The oil level is correct."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["A mechanical gauge replaces the oil pressure switch. When cold, the pressure is 3.5 bar at idle. When hot, it is 0.7 bar at idle and 2.5 bar at 3,000 rpm.","The manual says that pressure at hot idle should be at least 1.0 bar. The pressure is too low.","The oil is thin and smells of fuel, so it is changed, and the filter. The pressure improves only a little.","The sump is removed. The pickup strainer is clear and the pump gears are within limits. The oil pressure relief valve moves freely.","The main bearings are inspected. Clearances are above the limit, so the bearings are worn and are the cause of the pressure loss when hot.","The customer is told the engine needs new bearings and probably a crankshaft regrind. A new engine is also offered as an option."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What the cases teach"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Start with the complaint and a visual check, then the simplest test.","Compare a reading with the specification, and with the other cylinders or states.","Change only one thing at a time and test again.","Confirm the fault by seeing it, not only by inference.","Record each reading. Written results justify a repair and protect you if the customer has questions."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Diagnosis is a method, not a guess. Each test should rule something in or out. If it does not, you have chosen the wrong test."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never run a test that could hurt the engine further. If oil pressure is very low, or the engine overheats, stop at once and investigate before you run it again."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Compression test and cooling system pressure test","objective":"Perform and record a compression test on a petrol engine and a pressure test on its cooling system, then interpret the results against the specification.","time":"90 minutes","tools":["Compression tester with adaptors","Cooling system pressure tester and cap adaptor","Spark plug socket, ratchet, torque wrench and a blower or rag","Workshop manual or specification sheet","Notepad and a results table"],"safety":["Never open the radiator or expansion tank cap on a hot engine.","Disable the fuel and ignition before cranking with the plugs removed, so fuel is not sprayed and no coil sparks.","Keep hands, hair and clothing away from belts and fans.","Stand clear of the spark plug holes, as debris can be blown out.","Do not exceed the pressure on the radiator cap during the cooling system test."],"steps":["Find the compression specification, the firing order and the cap pressure in the manual.","Warm the engine, switch it off and disable the fuel pump and the ignition as the manual directs.","Clean around each spark plug, remove all plugs and keep them in order.","Open the throttle fully and fit the compression tester to cylinder 1.","Crank the engine for four to five compression strokes and note the highest reading.","Repeat for each cylinder and record every reading in your table.","Work out the difference between the highest and lowest cylinders as a percentage.","If one cylinder is low, do a wet test with a small amount of oil and note the change.","Refit the plugs to the specified torque, reconnect the fuel and ignition and let the engine cool fully.","With the engine cold, fit the cooling system pressure tester and pump to the cap rating.","Watch the gauge for two minutes and note any drop, and look for leaks.","Release the pressure slowly, refit the cap and write a short diagnosis from all the results."],"checks":["I recorded a compression reading for every cylinder.","I calculated the difference between cylinders and compared it with the specification.","I carried out a wet test and explained the result.","I pressure tested the cooling system at the correct pressure without exceeding it.","I wrote a diagnosis based on measured results, not guesses."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m05-q01","q":"Which is NOT one of the four jobs of engine oil?","options":["Holding dirt in suspension until the filter removes it","Lubricating the moving parts of the engine","Making the fuel burn faster","Cooling parts such as pistons and bearings"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l1","explain":"Oil lubricates, cools, cleans and seals. It does not change how fast the fuel burns."},
    {"id":"m05-q02","q":"What does the oil pressure relief valve do?","options":["It keeps the oil inside the engine when the vehicle is parked for a long time","It opens when the oil level in the sump falls too low to supply the pump","It lets oil flow back into the filter housing once the engine stops running","It returns surplus oil to the sump when the pressure rises too high"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l1","explain":"The relief valve stops the pressure going too high, for example when the oil is cold and thick."},
    {"id":"m05-q03","q":"What is the purpose of the oil filter bypass valve?","options":["To let oil reach the engine even if the filter is blocked","To make the oil flow faster through a clean filter at all times","To reduce the oil pressure when the engine is running at idle","To drain the filter into the sump when the engine is switched off"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l1","explain":"Unfiltered oil is better than no oil. The valve opens when the filter cannot pass enough flow."},
    {"id":"m05-q04","q":"The oil pressure warning light comes on while you are driving. What should you do?","options":["Ignore the light until the temperature gauge starts to rise as well","Stop safely, switch off the engine and check the oil level","Increase the engine speed to raise the oil pressure back to normal","Drive on carefully to the nearest workshop at a low engine speed"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l1","explain":"Low oil pressure can wreck bearings in minutes. Stop and investigate."},
    {"id":"m05-q05","q":"What does the oil pressure warning light actually show?","options":["That the oil level in the sump is exactly at the minimum","That the oil is due to be changed at the next service","That oil pressure is below the safe minimum","That the oil filter is completely blocked"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l1","explain":"It reports pressure, not level. An engine can have the correct level and still have low pressure."},
    {"id":"m05-q06","q":"What problem does the oil pump relief valve prevent when the oil is cold and thick?","options":["Air entering the oil pump and making it lose its prime","Excess pressure that could burst the filter or seals","Metal particles passing from the sump into the main gallery","The oil level in the sump rising above the full mark"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l1","explain":"The pump moves more oil as speed rises. The relief valve opens at a set pressure and returns the surplus, which protects filters and seals."},
    {"id":"m05-q07","q":"What is the job of the anti-drain (non-return) valve in an oil filter?","options":["To stop oil flowing into the filter when the engine is hot","To release excess pressure from the main gallery","To let unfiltered oil past when the engine is cold","To stop oil draining out so bearings get oil at start-up"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l2","explain":"With the valve closed, oil stays in the filter when the engine stops. The pump then has oil ready to supply the bearings at once on the next start."},
    {"id":"m05-q08","q":"A leak in an oil-to-water cooler can cause which of these?","options":["Coolant mixing with the oil, or the reverse","A fall in the tyre pressure on the front wheels","A rise in the battery voltage when starting","A loss of brake pedal pressure at low speed"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l2","explain":"The cooler holds oil and coolant on opposite sides of a thin wall. A leak lets them mix, giving milky oil or an oily film in the coolant tank."},
    {"id":"m05-q09","q":"Why does a turbocharger oil drain pipe need to be free of kinks and blockages?","options":["The turbocharger needs oil to flow upward to cool it","The drain pipe also supplies the boost pressure to the engine","Oil that cannot drain backs up and leaks into the turbine","A kinked pipe makes the exhaust gas flow in reverse"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l2","explain":"The turbo spins at very high speed on a film of oil. If the oil cannot drain freely, it fills the bearing housing and leaks past the seals."},
    {"id":"m05-q10","q":"The oil on the dipstick looks like milky coffee. What does this suggest?","options":["The oil has been in the engine for too short a time to mix properly","The oil filter has been fitted incorrectly and is letting air in","The engine has been run with far too much oil in the sump","Coolant is mixing with the oil, perhaps through a head gasket"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l3","explain":"Milky oil points to coolant contamination. Test the cooling system and the gasket."},
    {"id":"m05-q11","q":"Oil on the dipstick smells of fuel and is thin. What is the likely cause?","options":["Fuel is leaking into the oil, for example from rich running or too many cold starts","The engine is using the wrong grade of coolant for its cooling system","The oil has overheated and the additives in it have burned off completely","The air filter is partially blocked and is starving the engine of air"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l3","explain":"Fuel dilutes oil and lowers its viscosity. Find the source of the fuel."},
    {"id":"m05-q12","q":"What is blow-by?","options":["Fuel that flows back from the injector into the tank after shutdown","Air that is blown through the radiator by the cooling fan at speed","Exhaust gas that is returned to the intake on purpose to cut emissions","Combustion gas that leaks past the piston rings into the crankcase"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l3","explain":"Some blow-by is normal. The PCV system removes it from the crankcase."},
    {"id":"m05-q13","q":"What does the PCV system do?","options":["It cools the oil before it returns to the sump from the cylinder head","It filters the oil as it leaves the pump and goes to the bearings","It draws crankcase gases into the intake so they are burned","It pumps clean air into the sump to cool the oil in hot weather"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l3","explain":"A blocked PCV can raise crankcase pressure and push oil out of seals."},
    {"id":"m05-q14","q":"What does blue smoke from the exhaust usually mean?","options":["The engine is only warming up and the smoke will clear soon","Coolant is burning in the cylinders because of a leaking gasket","The engine is being given too much fuel by the injectors","Oil is being burned in the combustion chambers"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l3","explain":"Blue smoke is a sign of oil burning, from worn rings or valve stem seals."},
    {"id":"m05-q15","q":"A used oil report shows high copper and lead. What does this suggest?","options":["Dirt getting past the air filter","Piston wear in an aluminium piston","Water getting into the fuel system","Bearing wear or a leaking cooler"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l3","explain":"Bearings contain copper and lead alloys. High levels suggest the bearings are wearing. Silicon would suggest dirt, and aluminium would suggest piston wear."},
    {"id":"m05-q16","q":"What is the first step when measuring oil consumption?","options":["Drain half of the oil to see how quickly it is used","Remove the oil filter and run the engine without it","Fill to the full mark and record the odometer","Add an extra litre of oil over the full mark"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l3","explain":"A measurement needs a known starting point. Fill to the full mark, record the distance and check again after a set distance in the same conditions."},
    {"id":"m05-q17","q":"Why is aggressive chemical flushing of a sludged engine risky?","options":["Loosened sludge can block oil passages","It makes the oil colour turn from black to brown","It stops the oil filter from working at all","It raises the oil pressure above the safe range"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l3","explain":"Harsh chemicals can break sludge off in lumps that jam the pickup strainer or galleries. Gentle flushing and frequent oil changes are safer."},
    {"id":"m05-q18","q":"Why must a pressure cap be fitted to the cooling system?","options":["It makes the coolant circulate faster through the radiator at high speed","It keeps dirt and insects from entering the expansion tank on the road","It stops air from entering the system when the engine is cooling down again","It raises the boiling point so the coolant does not boil at normal running temperature"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l4","explain":"A pressurised system boils at a higher temperature than an open one."},
    {"id":"m05-q19","q":"What is the main job of the antifreeze in coolant?","options":["To make the coolant thicker so that it flows slower through the engine","To clean the radiator tubes by slowly dissolving any deposits in the metal","To make the engine reach its working temperature much faster in the cold","To lower the freezing point, raise the boiling point and protect against corrosion"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l4","explain":"Plain water is a poor coolant in an engine. The additives protect the metals."},
    {"id":"m05-q20","q":"When can you safely open the radiator cap?","options":["Only when the engine is cold, opening it slowly and covering it with a cloth","When the temperature gauge reads in the middle of the normal range","After the heater has been switched on at full for a few minutes","At any time, as long as the engine is only idling and the fan is running"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l4","explain":"A hot engine holds coolant above its boiling point under pressure. Open it only when cold."},
    {"id":"m05-q21","q":"What does the radiator do?","options":["It controls the temperature at which the engine runs once it is warm","It pumps the coolant around the engine at all engine speeds and loads","It passes engine heat from the coolant to the air through its tubes and fins","It stores the extra coolant that the engine does not need while it is cold"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l4","explain":"The radiator is a heat exchanger between the coolant and the air."},
    {"id":"m05-q22","q":"Why should a radiator cap never be opened on a hot engine?","options":["The cap is too hot to hold with bare hands for long","Coolant above boiling point can spray out","The coolant would be drained out of the radiator","The engine would lose its oil pressure quickly"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l4","explain":"The system is pressurised, which raises the boiling point. Opening the cap drops the pressure, and the coolant flashes into steam and can scald you."},
    {"id":"m05-q23","q":"What does the vacuum valve in a pressure cap do?","options":["It lets air out of the system when it is first filled","It opens at high temperature to release steam to air","It lets coolant return as the system cools","It controls the speed of the cooling fan motor"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l5","explain":"As the engine cools, the coolant contracts and the system would draw a vacuum. The vacuum valve opens to let coolant return from the expansion tank."},
    {"id":"m05-q24","q":"An engine sends 50 kW into the coolant, which should rise 8 °C. The specific heat is 3.5 kJ/kg·K. About what flow of coolant is needed?","options":["About 0.18 kg/s","About 1.8 kg/s","About 18 kg/s","About 6.25 kg/s"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l5","explain":"Flow = heat ÷ (specific heat × temperature rise) = 50 ÷ (3.5 × 8) = 1.79 kg/s, around 100 litres per minute."},
    {"id":"m05-q25","q":"What does a leak from the weep hole of a water pump show?","options":["The pump seal has failed","The coolant has the wrong mixture","The thermostat is stuck open","The radiator cap is too weak"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l5","explain":"The weep hole lets a leaking seal show itself before coolant reaches the bearing. A leak means the pump is on its way out."},
    {"id":"m05-q26","q":"Why does a lower radiator hose often have a spring inside?","options":["To heat the coolant before it enters the engine","To make the hose easier to bend round tight corners","To prevent the clamp from slipping off the end","To stop it collapsing under the suction of the pump"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l5","explain":"The pump draws coolant through this hose. A weak or soft hose can collapse flat at high speed and cause overheating that disappears when the engine slows."},
    {"id":"m05-q27","q":"How does a wax thermostat open?","options":["The water pump turns the valve open by a mechanical linkage and a spring","The coolant pressure forces the valve open once the pump reaches a set speed","A sensor tells the computer to open the valve with a small electric motor","The wax melts and expands as the coolant heats, pushing the valve open"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l6","explain":"The wax element expands at a set temperature and opens the valve."},
    {"id":"m05-q28","q":"A thermostat is stuck closed. What is the most likely symptom?","options":["The engine takes a very long time to warm up after a cold start","The engine overheats soon after starting","The heater blows cold air into the cabin all the time","The engine does not start at all on a cold morning"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l6","explain":"Coolant cannot reach the radiator, so heat builds up."},
    {"id":"m05-q29","q":"A thermostat is stuck open. What is the most likely symptom?","options":["Rapid overheating at idle with steam escaping from the radiator cap","A blown head gasket after only a short time of driving","Slow warm-up and a temperature gauge that reads low","A steam leak from the pressure cap on a hot day in traffic"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l6","explain":"Coolant flows to the radiator all the time, so the engine runs cool."},
    {"id":"m05-q30","q":"Why can a faulty coolant temperature sensor cause a fuel problem?","options":["The engine computer uses it to set the amount of fuel","The sensor changes the spark plug gap when the engine is hot","The sensor is a part of the fuel filter housing near the tank","The sensor is wired directly to the fuel pump and switches it on"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l6","explain":"A wrong signal makes the computer use the wrong mixture, often rich."},
    {"id":"m05-q31","q":"The upper radiator hose stays cool while the engine overheats. What is the most likely fault?","options":["A thermostat stuck open","A thermostat stuck closed","A leaking radiator cap","A faulty windscreen washer pump"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l6","explain":"If the thermostat stays shut, hot coolant never reaches the radiator, so the upper hose stays cool while the engine gets hotter."},
    {"id":"m05-q32","q":"A coolant temperature sensor wire breaks and goes open circuit. What does the computer think?","options":["That it is very cold, since resistance is high","That the engine is very hot, because the voltage is zero","That the engine is at normal temperature","That the thermostat has been removed"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l6","explain":"An NTC sensor has high resistance when cold. An open circuit looks like infinite resistance, so the computer enriches the mixture and a code is set."},
    {"id":"m05-q33","q":"A cooling system pressure test is done at what pressure?","options":["The pressure stamped on the cap, with the system cold","At any pressure that the tester can reach, with the engine hot","At zero pressure with the engine running and the fan on","At twice the cap pressure, to find the smaller leaks more quickly"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l7","explain":"Never go above the cap rating. A higher pressure can split hoses and damage the radiator."},
    {"id":"m05-q34","q":"After a pressure test, the gauge slowly falls but there are no drips. What should you suspect?","options":["A flat battery that is causing the pump to run slowly","An internal leak, such as a head gasket","A fault in the temperature gauge that makes it read low","A thermostat fitted the wrong way round in its housing"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l7","explain":"Coolant leaking into a cylinder or the oil does not show as an external drip."},
    {"id":"m05-q35","q":"Which sign points to a head gasket failure?","options":["Bubbles in the coolant and a falling level without any external leak","Oil on the outside of the radiator hose and a squealing belt at start-up","A heater that works only when the engine is cold and stops when hot","Only a belt that squeals when the engine is first started up"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l7","explain":"Combustion gas enters the cooling system and pushes coolant out."},
    {"id":"m05-q36","q":"The temperature gauge rises while you are driving. What is the best immediate action?","options":["Open the radiator cap straight away to let the steam out of the system","Rev the engine hard to make the fan and the pump cool it faster","Switch off the A/C, turn the heater on full and stop safely as soon as you can","Keep driving to the workshop at the same speed as before"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l7","explain":"The heater draws extra heat from the engine. Then stop and let it cool before checking."},
    {"id":"m05-q37","q":"A cooling system pressure test is done on a cold engine. How should the pump pressure be set?","options":["To twice the pressure stamped on the cap","To zero, so the system is not damaged","To the pressure shown on the tyre placard","To the cap rating, never above it"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l7","explain":"Going above the cap rating can split hoses or damage the radiator. The system should hold the pressure for a couple of minutes."},
    {"id":"m05-q38","q":"Which finding most strongly suggests a head gasket leak into the cooling system?","options":["A dirty air filter in the filter housing","Low windscreen washer fluid in the bottle","Exhaust gas found in the coolant","A worn drive belt on the alternator"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l7","explain":"A block test changes colour when exhaust gas is present in the coolant. Other signs include bubbles in the tank and white smoke."},
    {"id":"m05-q39","q":"After an engine has overheated badly, what should be checked even if it seems to run normally?","options":["Coolant, oil, compression and head flatness","Only the colour of the temperature gauge needle","Only the tyre pressures and wheel balance","Only the radio and the cabin lights"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l7","explain":"Overheating can warp the head and damage the gasket and pistons. Check for coolant in the oil and measure compression before you tell the customer all is well."},
    {"id":"m05-q40","q":"Why must a thermostat be fitted the correct way round?","options":["The colour of the thermostat shows its working direction","A wrong thermostat can start the car engine on its own","The temperature is stamped on the other side only","Its valve and air bleed face a set way"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l8","explain":"A thermostat has a spring side and a jiggle valve that must be at the top. A backwards fit can stop it working or trap air."},
    {"id":"m05-q41","q":"After refilling a cooling system, what is the correct way to remove air?","options":["Fill it to the top and drive straight away","Remove the thermostat and run without it","Bleed with the heater on hot and top up","Run the engine with the cap on and the heater off"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l8","explain":"Air trapped in the engine or heater matrix gives hot spots and poor heating. Follow the manual's bleeding procedure and recheck when cold."},
    {"id":"m05-q42","q":"When a radiator is replaced on a car with an air-conditioning condenser fixed to it, what must not be done?","options":["Disconnect the battery before starting work","Open the refrigerant circuit without equipment","Drain the coolant before disconnecting the hoses","Transfer rubber mounts to the new radiator"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l8","explain":"The condenser can be unbolted and moved aside while still connected. Opening the refrigerant lines releases refrigerant to the air, which is illegal and unsafe."},
    {"id":"m05-q43","q":"What should you do before a compression test?","options":["Fill each cylinder with oil through the plug holes before cranking","Warm the engine, disable fuel and ignition, and remove all spark plugs","Leave the engine cold and keep all of the spark plugs in place","Disconnect the oil pressure sensor and the temperature sensor first"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"The engine should be warm and cranking freely. Disabling the fuel and ignition keeps the test safe."},
    {"id":"m05-q44","q":"One cylinder has low compression. A little oil squirted in makes the reading rise. What does this suggest?","options":["A cracked spark plug insulator in the cylinder head","Worn piston rings","A burnt exhaust valve that is leaking compression in that cylinder","A failed head gasket between this cylinder and the next one"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"Oil temporarily seals the worn rings and raises the reading. A valve or gasket fault would not change."},
    {"id":"m05-q45","q":"Two neighbouring cylinders both have very low compression. What is the likely fault?","options":["Worn rings on both pistons that happen to have worn at the same time","A leaking head gasket between the cylinders","A weak battery that cranks the engine too slowly to be accurate","A blocked fuel filter that is restricting the fuel to both cylinders"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"The gasket between adjacent cylinders can leak from one to the other."},
    {"id":"m05-q46","q":"In a leak-down test, air hisses from the exhaust pipe. Where is the leak?","options":["Past the exhaust valve","Through the head gasket into the coolant","Past the intake valve","Past the piston rings"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"Hissing at the exhaust means that the exhaust valve is not sealing."},
    {"id":"m05-q47","q":"What does bubbling in the expansion tank during a leak-down test suggest?","options":["The thermostat is stuck closed and the coolant is under pressure","A leak through the head gasket into the coolant","The oil is too thin and is leaking past the engine seals","The valve clearance is too large and the valves are not sealing"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"Air escaping into the coolant means a gasket or cracked head."},
    {"id":"m05-q48","q":"A deep rhythmic knock that gets louder under load usually indicates what?","options":["A noisy valve adjuster that has been set with too much clearance","A loose drive belt that is slipping on the alternator pulley","A worn big-end or main bearing","A cracked exhaust manifold that is leaking hot gas into the engine bay"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"Bearing knock is deep and load related, and the oil pressure is often low."},
    {"id":"m05-q49","q":"What does a vacuum gauge needle that flutters at idle suggest?","options":["A failed cooling fan relay or a faulty temperature sensor","A valve, ignition or leak problem that needs further tests","A perfectly healthy engine that is breathing well at idle","A blocked fuel tank vent that is stopping the fuel from flowing"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"A steady needle means a healthy engine. Fluttering needs a closer look."},
    {"id":"m05-q50","q":"White smoke with a sweet smell from the exhaust points to what?","options":["Coolant burning in the cylinders","A blocked air filter","Oil burning past worn piston rings","Fuel burning because of a rich mixture"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"Burning coolant produces white smoke and a sweet smell."},
    {"id":"m05-q51","q":"A compression test shows 12, 12, 6 and 12 bar. A wet test does not change the 6 bar reading. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Worn piston rings in that same cylinder","A weak battery that gives slow cranking","A blocked air filter on the engine intake","A leaking valve or damaged seat"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"Oil temporarily seals worn rings. If the reading does not rise, the leak is past the valve or the gasket."},
    {"id":"m05-q52","q":"A vacuum gauge needle drops regularly to a low value and then returns. What does this suggest?","options":["A blocked fuel filter on the supply line","A burnt or sticking valve","A weak battery when starting the engine","A worn drive belt on the alternator"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"A valve that fails to seal gives a regular dip every time it comes round. A steady low reading would suggest late timing or an air leak."},
    {"id":"m05-q53","q":"What does a leak-down test show that a compression test does not?","options":["How much oil the engine burns in a thousand kilometres","The octane rating of the fuel in the tank","Where the leak is, such as valve, rings or gasket","The temperature at which the thermostat opens"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m05-l9","explain":"A leak-down test feeds air into the cylinder and lets you hear it escape. A hiss at the exhaust means a valve, at the oil filler means rings, and bubbles in the coolant mean a gasket."},
    {"id":"m05-q54","q":"Why is it a good step to swap a coil to a different cylinder when tracing a misfire?","options":["Whether the fault follows the coil or the cylinder","It makes the engine run on the remaining three cylinders","It resets the fault code stored by the computer","It cleans the contacts of the connector and the plug"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m05-l10","explain":"If the misfire moves with the coil, the coil is faulty. If it stays, the cause is elsewhere, such as the plug, injector or compression."},
    {"id":"m05-q55","q":"An engine overheats in slow traffic only, and runs cool at speed. What does this suggest first?","options":["A worn crankshaft bearing in the lower engine","A cooling fan or fan control fault","A blocked injector on cylinder number two","A leaking brake pipe near the rear wheel"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l10","explain":"At speed the vehicle forces air through the radiator. In slow traffic the fan has to do it, so a fan fault, relay, switch or sensor is the first suspect."},
    {"id":"m05-q56","q":"An engine has 0.7 bar oil pressure at hot idle, and the manual limit is 1.0 bar. The oil level, pump and relief valve are good. What is the likely cause?","options":["Excess bearing clearance","A stuck-open thermostat","An overfilled washer bottle","A blocked cabin filter"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m05-l10","explain":"As the oil thins when hot, worn bearings with large clearances let it escape too fast, so pressure falls even though the pump is good."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m05-c01","front":"What are the four jobs of engine oil?","back":"Lubricate, cool, clean and seal.","lesson":"m05-l1"},
    {"id":"m05-c02","front":"What does the pressure relief valve do?","back":"It returns surplus oil to the sump when the pressure is too high.","lesson":"m05-l1"},
    {"id":"m05-c03","front":"What does the filter bypass valve do?","back":"It lets oil reach the engine if the filter is blocked.","lesson":"m05-l1"},
    {"id":"m05-c04","front":"What should you do when the oil pressure warning light comes on?","back":"Stop safely, switch off the engine and find the cause.","lesson":"m05-l1"},
    {"id":"m05-c05","front":"What are the three common types of oil pump?","back":"Gear pump, gerotor (rotor) pump and vane pump. Variable-displacement types reduce flow at speed.","lesson":"m05-l2"},
    {"id":"m05-c06","front":"What does an oil cooler do and what is its risk?","back":"It removes heat from the oil. A leak in an oil-to-water cooler mixes oil and coolant.","lesson":"m05-l2"},
    {"id":"m05-c07","front":"Typical hot oil pressures?","back":"About 1 to 2 bar at idle and 3 to 5 bar at higher speed. Check the manual.","lesson":"m05-l2"},
    {"id":"m05-c08","front":"What does milky oil suggest?","back":"Coolant is mixing with the oil.","lesson":"m05-l3"},
    {"id":"m05-c09","front":"What does thin oil that smells of fuel suggest?","back":"Fuel is leaking into the oil.","lesson":"m05-l3"},
    {"id":"m05-c10","front":"What is blow-by?","back":"Combustion gas that leaks past the piston rings into the crankcase.","lesson":"m05-l3"},
    {"id":"m05-c11","front":"What does the PCV system do?","back":"It removes crankcase gases and burns them in the engine.","lesson":"m05-l3"},
    {"id":"m05-c12","front":"What does blue exhaust smoke mean?","back":"Oil is burning in the cylinders.","lesson":"m05-l3"},
    {"id":"m05-c13","front":"What does high silicon in an oil analysis suggest?","back":"Dirt getting into the engine, perhaps through the air filter or an intake leak.","lesson":"m05-l3"},
    {"id":"m05-c14","front":"How do you measure oil consumption?","back":"Fill to the full mark, drive a set distance, then add measured oil back to the full mark.","lesson":"m05-l3"},
    {"id":"m05-c15","front":"What does a radiator do?","back":"It transfers heat from the coolant to the air.","lesson":"m05-l4"},
    {"id":"m05-c16","front":"What does a pressure cap do?","back":"It raises the boiling point of the coolant by holding the system under pressure.","lesson":"m05-l4"},
    {"id":"m05-c17","front":"When can the radiator cap be opened?","back":"Only when the engine is cold.","lesson":"m05-l4"},
    {"id":"m05-c18","front":"Why must coolant types not be mixed?","back":"They can react and form deposits that block passages.","lesson":"m05-l4"},
    {"id":"m05-c19","front":"What does the pressure cap vacuum valve do?","back":"It opens as the engine cools to let coolant flow back from the expansion tank.","lesson":"m05-l5"},
    {"id":"m05-c20","front":"Formula for heat carried by coolant?","back":"Heat = mass flow × specific heat × temperature rise. Specific heat of coolant is about 3.5 kJ/kg·K.","lesson":"m05-l5"},
    {"id":"m05-c21","front":"What shows that a water pump seal has failed?","back":"A leak from the weep hole in the pump housing.","lesson":"m05-l5"},
    {"id":"m05-c22","front":"Three types of cooling fan?","back":"Viscous (engine-driven), electric with a relay or switch, and variable-speed electric.","lesson":"m05-l5"},
    {"id":"m05-c23","front":"How does a wax thermostat work?","back":"The wax melts and expands with heat and pushes the valve open.","lesson":"m05-l6"},
    {"id":"m05-c24","front":"What is the symptom of a thermostat stuck closed?","back":"Overheating soon after the engine starts.","lesson":"m05-l6"},
    {"id":"m05-c25","front":"What is the symptom of a thermostat stuck open?","back":"Slow warm-up and a low temperature reading.","lesson":"m05-l6"},
    {"id":"m05-c26","front":"How can a faulty coolant sensor affect the engine?","back":"The computer uses it to set fuel quantity, so the engine may run rich.","lesson":"m05-l6"},
    {"id":"m05-c27","front":"How does an NTC coolant sensor behave?","back":"Resistance falls as the engine warms. An open circuit looks very cold and a short looks very hot.","lesson":"m05-l6"},
    {"id":"m05-c28","front":"What is an electronically controlled thermostat?","back":"A wax thermostat with a heater the computer can switch on to open the valve early under load.","lesson":"m05-l6"},
    {"id":"m05-c29","front":"At what pressure is a cooling system tested?","back":"At the cap's rated pressure, with the engine cold.","lesson":"m05-l7"},
    {"id":"m05-c30","front":"What are signs of a head gasket failure?","back":"Bubbles in the coolant, white smoke, milky oil and coolant loss without a leak.","lesson":"m05-l7"},
    {"id":"m05-c31","front":"How do you bleed air from a cooling system?","back":"Follow the manual: use the bleed screw or run the engine with the heater on hot and top up.","lesson":"m05-l7"},
    {"id":"m05-c32","front":"What happens after a bad overheat?","back":"The head can warp, the gasket can fail and pistons can scuff. Check coolant, oil, compression and head flatness.","lesson":"m05-l7"},
    {"id":"m05-c33","front":"Which way does a thermostat go in?","back":"With the correct side to the engine and the jiggle valve or air bleed at the top.","lesson":"m05-l8"},
    {"id":"m05-c34","front":"What is a reverse flush?","back":"Water pushed through the radiator against the normal flow to shift deposits from the tubes.","lesson":"m05-l8"},
    {"id":"m05-c35","front":"How is a compression test done?","back":"Warm engine, fuel and ignition disabled, plugs out, throttle open, crank and record each cylinder.","lesson":"m05-l9"},
    {"id":"m05-c36","front":"What does a wet test show?","back":"If the reading rises after adding oil, the piston rings are worn.","lesson":"m05-l9"},
    {"id":"m05-c37","front":"What does a leak-down test measure?","back":"The percentage of air that escapes from the cylinder, and where it goes.","lesson":"m05-l9"},
    {"id":"m05-c38","front":"What does a steady vacuum gauge reading at idle show?","back":"A healthy engine that breathes well.","lesson":"m05-l9"},
    {"id":"m05-c39","front":"What is a deep rhythmic knock under load?","back":"A worn big-end or main bearing.","lesson":"m05-l9"},
    {"id":"m05-c40","front":"What does white smoke with a sweet smell mean?","back":"Coolant is burning in the cylinders.","lesson":"m05-l9"},
    {"id":"m05-c41","front":"Compression low on one cylinder, wet test no change?","back":"A leaking valve, a burnt valve or a damaged valve seat.","lesson":"m05-l9"},
    {"id":"m05-c42","front":"What does a vacuum gauge needle that dips regularly mean?","back":"A burnt or sticking valve.","lesson":"m05-l9"},
    {"id":"m05-c43","front":"Why swap a coil to another cylinder?","back":"To see whether the misfire follows the coil or stays with the cylinder.","lesson":"m05-l10"},
    {"id":"m05-c44","front":"Overheating only in slow traffic points to what?","back":"A cooling air flow problem, such as the fan, its relay, switch or sensor.","lesson":"m05-l10"}
  ]
});
